#
# Re-protect every secret of an imported key, however busy the server.
# gpg's 'passwd' re-protects each secret through gpg-agent's PASSWD.
# After the first, the agent keeps the old and new passphrases under
# cache nonces and answers the later secrets itself, but those entries
# expire with max-cache-ttl, which the server sets to 1 s. When gpg
# reaches the next secret after that, the agent asks for its old
# passphrase again. The responder answered every prompt after the first
# old passphrase with the new one, because want_new_passphrase never went
# back to False, so that secret stayed on its old passphrase and the
# import failed with IMPORT_PASSPHRASE_ERROR. The responder now tracks
# which passphrase the next prompt asks for: the new one only straight
# after the old one was accepted. want_new_passphrase keeps its meaning
# for the final state check (#189).
#
# The agent reads the cached old passphrase before it decrypts the next
# secret and the new one after, so it can lose the new one alone and ask
# for that only. gpg relays both requests as the same prompt, so the
# responder answers it with the old passphrase, and the secret stays on
# it although the session succeeds (#190). The responder then expects the
# new passphrase next, so it answers a later secret's old-passphrase
# prompt with the new one, and that secret fails the session. After a
# session that answered more than two prompts, each secret the old
# passphrase still unlocks is re-protected through the agent directly,
# which names each request, and every secret present must then accept
# the new passphrase. A complete key whose session failed is accepted
# once that check has passed. This is the cause patch 27's log was added
# to confirm; its prompt list still records each answer. See
# patches/README.md.
#
